consideration: hotspot rectangles with fills=[] and no stroke are invisible but still receive clicks in prototype mode. Yes — standard practice (\"hotspot\" layers). \n\nAlso set r.clipsContent irrelevant. \n\nEdge: rect on top of everything in z-order (appended last) — receives clicks. But the Nav Link instances already have MOUSE_ENTER hover reactions from component — hover still works since hotspot has no fills (does an invisible rect block hover on elements below? In Figma prototyping, a rect with no fill does NOT block interactions to layers below? Actually fills=[] layers are not hit-testable in prototype? Hmm — in Figma, layers with 0% opacity fills still receive clicks if \"hit area\"... Standard hotspot: a frame with no fill named e.g. \"Hotspot\" DOES work as a click target in Figma prototyping — that's exactly what the \"Add hotspot\" tool creates (a frame with no fill). And transparent overlays DO capture clicks on their area (that's why hotspots work over content). This means hover on nav underneath might be blocked within the hotspot area... The hotspot area = nav item area; hovering would trigger hotspot but nav hover variant switch happens on MOUSE_ENTER of the nav instance itself. If hotspot blocks mouse events to layers below, nav hover breaks.\n\nHmm. Trade-off: click navigation works, hover variant may not trigger underneath. In Figma, actually interactions: when overlapping, the TOPMOST interactive layer receives the event. Hotspot on top would swallow the hover. But nav hover would then never trigger — losing the hover states I built.\n\nAlternative: put hotspot BELOW nav in z-order? Then nav (instance with its own children) would swallow clicks... but nav instance itself has no ON_CLICK reaction (only hover). If a layer has interactions but no matching trigger for click, does click pass through to below? In Figma, if the top layer has no interactions at all, events pass to layers below. The nav instances HAVE interactions (hover). With ON_CLIC