aintenance issues, and the brief wants clean delivery. Hmm... but actually, is it that bad? A hidden duplicate would be confusing in the file.\n\n2. Alternative that MIGHT work: destination not ancestor — what if the reaction lives on the Nav Link COMPONENT MASTER (7:2 \"Current=No,State=Default\" variant) with dest 11:21? Masters are at PAGE ROOT → interactions on page-root nodes rejected (rect test). BUT — what if I move the component masters INSIDE a top-level frame (a \"Components\" container frame)? Components can live inside frames/sections! Then master-level reactions... wait but if I put the reaction ON the master variant (7:2) with dest 11:21, then EVERY instance of Current=No,State=Default (Work nav on Work page, Playground, Contact navs!) would navigate to 01_Work on click. Playground/Contact navs would wrongly navigate. BAD.\n\n   Unless... I create a SEPARATE component variant set for the \"Work\" nav specifically? Over-engineering.\n\n3. What about ON_CLICK on the header instance child \"Nav/Work\" but with destination 11:21 — rejected. FINAL.\n\n4. Hmm, actually — wait. What about setting the reaction on the TEXT Link masters... no.\n\n5. What about navigation \"NAVIGATE\" with `destinationId` = null and... no.\n\n6. **Check figma_ultralight actions**: maybe there's an action type like \"BACK\" — for the LOGO on Work page, BACK would return to previous page (e.g., if user came from About). For Work nav... BACK isn't right.\n\nHonestly, option 1 (document limitation) is reasonable — BUT let me reconsider once more whether the ancestor rejection might be avoidable via `figma.currentPage.selection`-based UI-level \"prototype\" — no.\n\nHmm, actually — one more: what does the error check? \"destination node may not exist\". What if the check is `destination.parent !== figma.currentPage`?? For 11:21, parent IS page 4:2... unless currentPage during the check is different! Wait — in my use_figma calls, is there a \"current page\" context? I did setCurrentPageAsync(4:2) in earlier calls but NOT in the wiring calls! In the wiring calls, figma.currentPage might default to page 0:1 (00 · Start Here)!! If validation requires destination.parent === currentPage, then dest 12:81/11:21 with parent 4:2 vs currentPage 0:1 → mismatch → \"may not exist\"!! But then why did aboutNav1 (dest 12:81) SUCCEED in the same call?? Same currentPage. Hmm — so no.\n\nUnless... aboutNav1 was set in the PREVIOUS call where I had... no, the successful wiring call didn't call setCurrentPageAsync either, and aboutNav1 succeeded. So currentPage theory fails.\n\nOK so it IS the ancestor rule: \"the destination may not [be an ancestor / contain this node]\". Actually the truncated text might read \"the destination node may not exist\" or \"may not [contain the source]\". Fine.\n\nFINAL DECISION on self-links: document as limitation. Actually WAIT — one more realistic trick used by Figma designers: link the Work nav & logo on 01_Work to 01_Work... in the UI this is allowed?? Actually I'm not 100% sure UI allows linking a frame to itself. Maybe UI ALSO disallows/requires flow starting point. In many Figma portfolio templates, self-links are done by duplicating... Actually in the Figma UI you CAN select a layer inside Frame A and set interaction → destination Frame A. I believe UI allows it. API rejects. Annoying.\n\nTrick to force it via API: set the reaction on a CLONE of the nav item temporarily placed OUTSIDE the frame, then MOVE the node into the frame? Moving a node with reactions into a frame — reactions survive moves! The validation happens at set-time. If I:\n1. Create hotspot rect ON THE PAGE ROOT? Interactions rejected at page root (rect test) — but that rejection was AT SET TIME. What if I set the reaction on a rect INSIDE 12:81 (dest 11:21 — non-ancestor, allowed!), then MOVE that rect into 11:21 over the Work nav? Moving preserves reactions (probably — reactions are node properties, not layout). Then the rect (now inside 11:21, over nav Work) has ON_CLICK → NAVIGATE 11:21. Validation passed at set time; does moving re-validate? Probably not. And at RUNTIME, Figma might tolerate self-destination (UI allows it).\n\n   Same for logo: hotspot over logo. And cue: set reaction on rect inside 12:81 with dest... cue wants SCROLL_TO 11:41 (not top-level → rejected at set time regardless of location). Hmm cue alternatives: skip cue click (not required by brief).\n\n   Plan:\n   a. In 12:81 (or any frame ≠ 11:21), create rect \"Hotspot / Work nav\" fills=[], set reactions → NAVIGATE 11:21 (allowed: rect inside 12:81, dest 11:21 non-ancestor).\n   b. Move rect: 11:21.appendChild(rect); rect.layoutPositioning=\"ABSOLUTE\"; position over the Nav/Work item (compute from absoluteBoundingBox).\n   c. Same for logo: rect2 set with dest 11:21 inside 12:81, then move into 11:21 over logo.\n   d. Verify: read back reactions after move (should persist), get positions right.\n\n   Risk: moving between frames may strip reactions? Reactions are overrides tied to the node — they survive reparenting in the plugin API (node.reactions is a property). Test empirically: after move, read rect.reactions and write to debug.\n\n   Positions: Nav/Work item inside header instance 11:22 at (0,0) in 11:21. Need absoluteBoundingBox of the Nav/Work instance: bb = navWork.absoluteBoundingBox (canvas coords). 11:21 at (0,3600). Hotspot x = bb.x - 0, y = bb.y - 3600. Size = bb.width × bb.height. Expand slightly: x-8, w+16? Nav item 39×36 — pad 6px all around for easier clicking. But don't overlap About nav (40px gap) too much. Pad 4.\n\n   Logo: text \"Logo\" inside header — bb gives position (~x=80, y=~30?, w~44, h~36).\n\n   ABSOLUTE positioning: after appendChild to 11:21 (auto-layout), set layoutPositioning=\"ABSOLUTE\", then set x,y — with ABSOLUTE, x/y are relative to the frame. Setting x/y after layoutPositioning works.\n\n   Hmm — wait, actually simpler: since I know the header layout (bar padding 0 80, logo left; nav right with 4 items